2. THE HONEST ENERGY MATH (no hype)
A kid's jump imparts roughly 300–600 joules of kinetic energy into the mat per bounce depending on weight and height, but piezoelectric conversion efficiency from mechanical flex is realistically 5–15%, not a full capture — so a single energetic bounce session yields on the order of tens of joules of usable electricity per jump, not hundreds. A full hour of enthusiastic multi-kid jumping might realistically generate 5–15 watt-hours total from the piezo array alone. That's not enough to power a house. It IS enough to:
Fully charge BH-06's LED ring for several nights of dusk-to-10pm operation
Trickle-charge a phone or small sensor node
Meaningfully offset a low-draw yard light or irrigation timer over a season
The solar skin (BH-02) is actually the larger energy contributor of the two on any sunny day — a trampoline's mat surface is a genuinely large flat area (10-14ft diameter), and thin-film solar at that footprint realistically produces 100-250 watt-hours per sunny day depending on film efficiency and angle. The honest framing: kid-bounce energy is the fun, engaging, small contributor. Solar-when-idle is the actual workhorse. Both are real. Neither is oversold here.
3. THE FLAIR — WHERE THIS GETS ACTUALLY BETTER
Base concept was "harvest the bounce, harvest the sun." Here's what makes it worth building:
3.1 Immediate feedback loop (the pedagogical hook)
BH-06's LED ring pulses brighter with every harvested bounce, in real time. Kids see their own jumping visibly making light happen. This is the opposite of algorithmic engagement — it's not manipulating attention, it's showing a true, direct, physical cause-and-effect relationship between their own body and a real energy outcome. That's a genuinely good thing for a kid to internalize early, and no screen or account required.
3.2 The analog dial, not an app
BH-07 is a physical, mechanical-style dial (like an odometer), not a screen or login. Shows "lightbulb-hours generated today" in plain kid-legible terms. Deliberately not gamified into a login-required app with notifications — it's Commons Kitchen's whole ethos applied here: engagement without extraction, no data collected, no account needed.
3.3 Night mode
After dark, BH-04's stored charge powers BH-06's LED ring at low glow — turns the trampoline into a visible, safe nighttime play marker in a backyard or park, powered entirely by what was harvested that same day. Nothing plugged in, nothing metered.
3.4 Fleet mode (park-scale deployment)
A playground with multiple BOUNCEHARVEST units can feed BH-04's battery banks into a shared low-voltage bus — same Nexareth-style confluence logic as the plaza spec — so a busy public playground's aggregate kid-energy-plus-solar output can genuinely offset the park's pathway lighting at night. This is where the "almost too easy" concept actually becomes infrastructure-relevant at scale, not just a novelty for one backyard.
4. INTEGRATION WITH EXISTING BEKW-CODE CANON
Solar skin (BH-02) shares flexible thin-film sourcing logic with VERDANTIA's transparent self-powered harvester panels.
Fleet-mode confluence bus (3.4) reuses NEXEDEN PLAZA's shared-grid architecture directly — same piezo-to-shared-grid pipeline, different kinetic source.
The "true cause-and-effect, no extraction" design principle in 3.1/3.2 is the same ethos underpinning Commons Kitchen.
End of spec. No license. No attribution required. Build it, break it, improve it, give it away again.
